---
// <BcnVizTip> — the ONE hover popup the whole dashboard micro-viz set uses. Wraps a
// mark (a column, a bar row, a legend row) and reads out a title plus one line per
// series: key stroke, label, value, optional note.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS (2026-09-09). The popup was built inside BcnVizStacked first, and
// four siblings needed the same thing: bcn-viz-trend, bcn-viz-bars, bcn-viz-donut,
// bcn-viz-meter. Copying ~40 lines of markup, ~30 of CSS and the same three CSS
// traps into five components is the copy-drift this repo's review explicitly hunts
// for, so the popup moved out here and BcnVizStacked now composes it. Every chart
// on the dashboard therefore agrees on where the panel sits, how a row is spaced,
// and which of the two readings is the strong one — by construction, not by five
// authors matching each other.
//
// TWO DATAVIZ RULES IT ENCODES, so no consumer has to remember them:
//  1. VALUES LEAD, LABELS FOLLOW. The reader already knows which series they are
//     pointing at; what they came for is the number. So the value is the strong,
//     high-contrast element and the label is secondary — the legend's hierarchy
//     deliberately inverted (a legend is scanned for identity, a popup for
//     magnitude).
//  2. THE KEY IS A CHIP AT THE FAR LEFT, then `label: value` (Jamie, 2026-09-09).
//     A chip matching the legend's own swatch, so the same shape means the same
//     thing in both places and a reader moving between them has nothing to relearn.
//     The row was briefly a coloured NUMBER with no chip — that put a series colour
//     on text, against the house rule that text wears text tokens, and it lost the
//     legend's visual rhyme. Both are back the way they were: colour identifies via
//     the chip, and every word and figure in the row is in ink.
//
// THE HIT TARGET IS WHATEVER THE CONSUMER SLOTS, and that is a rule with teeth:
// esa-popover's hover listeners live on its ANCHOR, which wraps the slotted trigger
// and is sized by it. Stretching this host does nothing on its own — measured, the
// host was 44px tall and the anchor inside it 2px, so a 2px column was still a 2px
// hover target. A chart whose mark is thin must therefore slot a FULL-HEIGHT CELL
// and bottom-align its mark inside that (see BcnVizTrend/BcnVizStacked's `__cell`),
// which is what makes the whole column of space hoverable. A chart whose mark
// already fills its row (bars, donut and meter legends) needs nothing extra.
//
// bcn-lego-checked: the popup IS esa-popover — composed, not reimplemented: it owns
// the hover/focus behaviour, the panel, the arrow and the positioning, and its
// REFLECTED `open` attribute is what BcnDashboardWidget keys
// `:has(esa-popover[open]) .esa-card { overflow: visible }` on, so choosing the
// lego is also what buys the popup its escape from esa-card's clipping. esa-tooltip
// was checked and does NOT fit: its content is a single `text` string, and this
// needs a line per series, which means real markup in slot="content". Checked
// Beacon prod: its dashboard widgets have no chart tooltip to port (the donut and
// bars there are static). This component adds ONLY the readout's structure and the
// host geometry around the lego — bcn-viz-tip is the reusable home for those.
export interface VizTipRow {
  /** The series or measure this line reads out. */
  label: string;
  /** Already formatted by the caller — this component never localises a number. */
  value: string | number;
  /** Series color, drawn as the chip at the row's left edge. Absent renders no chip
   *  — right for a single-series tip, where there is no identity to disambiguate. */
  color?: string;
  /** A second reading after the value, e.g. a share: "48%". */
  note?: string;
}

interface Props {
  /** The strong first line: a period, a category, a date. */
  title: string;
  /** One line per series. Empty falls through to `empty`. */
  rows?: VizTipRow[];
  /** Shown when `rows` is empty, e.g. "No observations". Absent renders nothing. */
  empty?: string;
  /** Panel side. 'top' (the default) is right for a chart sitting in a widget body. */
  position?: 'top' | 'bottom' | 'left' | 'right';
}

const { title, rows = [], empty, position = 'top' } = Astro.props;
---

<esa-popover class="bcn-viztip" trigger="hover" position={position}>
  <slot />

  <div slot="content" class="bcn-viztip__panel">
    <span class="bcn-viztip__title">{title}</span>
    {rows.length === 0 && empty && <span class="bcn-viztip__empty">{empty}</span>}
    {
      rows.map((row) => (
        <span class="bcn-viztip__row">
          {row.color && (
            <span class="bcn-viztip__key" style={`background:${row.color}`} aria-hidden="true" />
          )}
          <span class="bcn-viztip__label">{row.label}:</span>
          <span class="bcn-viztip__value">{row.value}</span>
          {row.note && <span class="bcn-viztip__note">{row.note}</span>}
        </span>
      ))
    }
  </div>
</esa-popover>

<script>
  // esa-popover is a Lit custom element, so the module has to be registered for the
  // tag to mean anything in the browser. Importing it here rather than in each
  // consumer means a chart gets the behaviour by composing this component, with no
  // second thing to remember.
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-popover';
</script>

<style>
  /* ── Host geometry. Three traps live here; all three were paid for once in
     BcnVizStacked and are now paid for in one place. ──

     1. Overriding esa-popover's `:host { display: inline-block }` from out here is
        legal — an outer-document rule beats :host on the same element — and it is
        the only way to make the host behave as a cell in the consumer's layout.
     2. GRID, not flex, and it is load-bearing. The trigger anchor lives in the
        popover's shadow root and cannot be selected from outside, so the only way
        to size it is through the layout its host imposes. As a flex container the
        host stretched the anchor vertically but left its width content-driven — and
        a slotted mark's `inline-size: 100%` then resolved against an auto width,
        i.e. against itself, collapsing to ZERO. Bars with height and no width:
        present, invisible. In grid, justify-items defaults to stretch, so the
        anchor fills the host on BOTH axes and a percentage has something real to
        resolve against.
     3. A PERCENTAGE HEIGHT ON A SLOTTED MARK STILL CANNOT WORK, because the anchor
        has no definite height. Consumers therefore pass their bars a PIXEL height
        (see BcnVizStacked/BcnVizTrend's STRIP_PX) — this component cannot fix that
        for them, so it is called out here rather than rediscovered per chart.
     4. AND A SLOTTED <span> IS NOT BLOCKIFIED. Whatever is slotted lands inside the
        anchor, not in the consumer's flex row, so a bare <span> stays display:inline
        and drops block-size entirely. BcnVizTrend's columns rendered 0x0 for exactly
        this reason. A slotted mark needs its own `display`. */
  .bcn-viztip {
    display: grid;
    /* Both axes stretch, so the anchor fills the host and the slotted trigger has a
       real box to fill in turn. No alignment knob: a chart that wants its mark on a
       baseline aligns it inside its OWN slotted cell, which is the only place the
       alignment can also carry the hit target. */
    justify-items: stretch;
    align-items: stretch;
    inline-size: 100%;
    /* Lets a truncating consumer (a bar row's ellipsised name) actually shrink. */
    min-inline-size: 0;
  }

  /* One line per series, so the panel is a small table rather than a sentence. */
  .bcn-viztip__panel {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-050);
    /* A popup sizes to its content; wrapping a series name would make it a
       paragraph floating over the chart. */
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .bcn-viztip__title {
    font-weight: var(--typography-font-weight-semibold, 600);
  }

  .bcn-viztip__row {
    display: flex;
    /* center, not baseline: the chip is a box with no text baseline of its own, so
       it has to align to the middle of the line it keys. */
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--spacing-100);
  }

  /* The key: the legend's own chip, at the row's left edge. */
  .bcn-viztip__key {
    inline-size: 8px;
    block-size: 8px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-xs);
    flex: none;
  }

  .bcn-viztip__label {
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary);
  }

  /* Left-aligned, immediately after the label and its colon (Jamie, 2026-09-09).
     It used to take `margin-inline-start: auto` so a multi-row panel read down a
     column of figures — which looked tidy and cost the panel its whole width in
     free space, pushing the number to the far right edge. On a chart in the third
     column that edge is the one the scroller clips, so the FIGURE was the part that
     got cut off: the row read "Concerns raised:" with nothing after it. A value that
     sits next to its label is both narrower and unclippable-by-accident.
     Ink, never the series colour — the chip on the left already carries identity. */
  .bcn-viztip__value {
    color: var(--color-content-default);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    font-weight: var(--typography-font-weight-semibold, 600);
  }

  /* The secondary reading — a share, a unit. Quiet, and never where the eye lands
     first: the count is the answer, this qualifies it. */
  .bcn-viztip__note {
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .bcn-viztip__empty {
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary);
  }
</style>
